<section id="A4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.4 </span>dLLM-Cache Configurations</h3>

<div id="A4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#A4.T4" title="In D.4 dLLM-Cache Configurations ‣ Appendix D Extended Ablation Study ‣ DyLLM: Efficient Diffusion LLM Inference via Saliency-based Token Selection and Partial Attention" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> details the configuration settings to reproduce the dLLM-cache baseline.
The hyperparameters <math id="A4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{P}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the prompt refresh interval and the response refresh interval, respectively.
The values of <math id="A4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{P}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> differ by an order of magnitude, resulting in frequent full computation over the response tokens, which might be helpful for long prompts.
We adopted the identical values reported in the original paper, given that the baseline relies on a static refresh policy, which necessitates dataset-specific tuning for optimal performance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A4.T4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 4</span>: </span><span id="A4.T4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Interval steps for LLaDA Instruct and Dream Instruct of dLLM-Cache</span></figcaption>
<table id="A4.T4.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A4.T4.5.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.T4.5.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="A4.T4.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model</span></td>
<td id="A4.T4.5.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="A4.T4.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Interval</span></td>
<td id="A4.T4.5.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A4.T4.5.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GSM8K</span></td>
<td id="A4.T4.5.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A4.T4.5.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MBPP</span></td>
<td id="A4.T4.5.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A4.T4.5.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Math</span></td>
<td id="A4.T4.5.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A4.T4.5.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MMLU-Pro</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T4.5.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.T4.5.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" rowspan="2"><span id="A4.T4.5.2.1.1" class="ltx_text">LLaDA 8B Instruct</span></td>
<td id="A4.T4.5.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><math id="A4.T4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{P}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A4.T4.5.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">50</td>
<td id="A4.T4.5.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">100</td>
<td id="A4.T4.5.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">50</td>
<td id="A4.T4.5.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">51</td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T4.5.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.T4.5.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="A4.T4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{R}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A4.T4.5.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">7</td>
<td id="A4.T4.5.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">7</td>
<td id="A4.T4.5.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">1</td>
<td id="A4.T4.5.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">3</td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T4.5.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.T4.5.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" rowspan="2"><span id="A4.T4.5.4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Dream 7B Instruct</span></td>
<td id="A4.T4.5.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><math id="A4.T4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{P}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A4.T4.5.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">25</td>
<td id="A4.T4.5.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">10</td>
<td id="A4.T4.5.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">50</td>
<td id="A4.T4.5.4.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">5</td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T4.5.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.T4.5.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><math id="A4.T4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{R}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A4.T4.5.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">2</td>
<td id="A4.T4.5.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">8</td>
<td id="A4.T4.5.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">1</td>
<td id="A4.T4.5.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">1</td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Formal Proofs of Propositions</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide the detailed mathematical derivations for the propositions presented in <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Salient Token Selection: Identifying Semantic Deltas ‣ 3 DyLLM ‣ DyLLM: Efficient Diffusion LLM Inference via Saliency-based Token Selection and Partial Attention" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>. These proofs establish the theoretical foundation for using temporal cosine similarity as a proxy for salient token selection in MDLMs.</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span>Proof of Proposition <a href="#S3.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ Proposition 3.1 (Scale Invariance under Linear Projection). ‣ 3.2 Salient Token Selection: Identifying Semantic Deltas ‣ 3 DyLLM ‣ DyLLM: Efficient Diffusion LLM Inference via Saliency-based Token Selection and Partial Attention" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></h3>

<div id="A5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1 (Scale Invariance under Linear Projection).</span> <span id="A5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> be the attention context, <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{o}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{o}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> be the output projection matrix, and <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\mathbb{R}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\mathbb{R}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> be a positive scaling factor. The composite operation of linear projection followed by RMSNorm satisfies: <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{RMSNorm}((\alpha C)W_{o})=\text{RMSNorm}(CW_{o})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">RMSNorm</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">RMSNorm</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{RMSNorm}((\alpha C)W_{o})=\text{RMSNorm}(CW_{o})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">By the linearity of the output projection <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{o}</annotation></semantics></math>, the projected representation scales linearly with the input:</span></p>
<table id="A5.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha C)W_{o}=\alpha(CW_{o})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha C)W_{o}=\alpha(CW_{o}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=CW_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=CW_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the projected vector. Recall the definition of the RMSNorm operation:</span></p>
<table id="A5.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{RMSNorm}(Y)=\frac{Y}{\text{RMS}(Y)}\odot\mathbf{g},\quad\text{where}\quad\text{RMS}(Y)=\sqrt{\frac{1}{d}\sum_{i=1}^{d}y_{i}^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>RMSNorm</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mtext>RMS</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mi>𝐠</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext>where</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>RMS</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{RMSNorm}(Y)=\frac{Y}{\text{RMS}(Y)}\odot\mathbf{g},\quad\text{where}\quad\text{RMS}(Y)=\sqrt{\frac{1}{d}\sum_{i=1}^{d}y_{i}^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">Applying this to the scaled vector <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha Y</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="A5.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{RMS}(\alpha Y)=\sqrt{\frac{1}{d}\sum(\alpha y_{i})^{2}}=\sqrt{\alpha^{2}\cdot\frac{1}{d}\sum y_{i}^{2}}=|\alpha|\text{RMS}(Y)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>RMS</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>α</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>RMS</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{RMS}(\alpha Y)=\sqrt{\frac{1}{d}\sum(\alpha y_{i})^{2}}=\sqrt{\alpha^{2}\cdot\frac{1}{d}\sum y_{i}^{2}}=|\alpha|\text{RMS}(Y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.p2.4.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\mathbb{R}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\mathbb{R}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\alpha|=\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\alpha|=\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. Substituting these into the RMSNorm formula:</span></p>
<table id="A5.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{RMSNorm}(\alpha Y)=\frac{\alpha Y}{\alpha\text{RMS}(Y)}\odot\mathbf{g}=\frac{Y}{\text{RMS}(Y)}\odot\mathbf{g}=\text{RMSNorm}(Y)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>RMSNorm</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>RMS</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mi>𝐠</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mtext>RMS</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mi>𝐠</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>RMSNorm</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{RMSNorm}(\alpha Y)=\frac{\alpha Y}{\alpha\text{RMS}(Y)}\odot\mathbf{g}=\frac{Y}{\text{RMS}(Y)}\odot\mathbf{g}=\text{RMSNorm}(Y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS1.p2.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.p2.5.1" class="ltx_text">Thus, <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{RMSNorm}((\alpha C)W_{o})=\text{RMSNorm}(CW_{o})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>RMSNorm</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>RMSNorm</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{RMSNorm}((\alpha C)W_{o})=\text{RMSNorm}(CW_{o})</annotation></semantics></math>, completing the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.2 </span>Proof of Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition 3.2 (Error Bound via Directional Alignment). ‣ 3.2 Salient Token Selection: Identifying Semantic Deltas ‣ 3 DyLLM ‣ DyLLM: Efficient Diffusion LLM Inference via Saliency-based Token Selection and Partial Attention" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a></h3>

<div id="A5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2 (Error Bound via Directional Alignment).</span>
<span id="A5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> error between normalized outputs,
<math id="A5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=\|\mathrm{RMSNorm}(Y_{t})-\mathrm{RMSNorm}(Y_{t-1})\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>RMSNorm</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>RMSNorm</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=\|\mathrm{RMSNorm}(Y_{t})-\mathrm{RMSNorm}(Y_{t-1})\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,
is bounded by the temporal cosine similarity <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t,l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t,l}</annotation></semantics></math> of the inputs as:</span></p>
<table id="A5.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\leq\kappa(W_{o})\sqrt{2(1-s_{t,l})}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\leq\kappa(W_{o})\sqrt{2(1-s_{t,l})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> be the unit-normalized input vectors (i.e., <math id="A5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}=C/\|C\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}=C/\|C\|</annotation></semantics></math>). The temporal cosine similarity is given by the dot product <math id="A5.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t,l}=\hat{C}_{t}^{\top}\hat{C}_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t,l}=\hat{C}_{t}^{\top}\hat{C}_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The Euclidean distance between these unit inputs is strictly determined by <math id="A5.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t,l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t,l}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="A5.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\hat{C}_{t}-\hat{C}_{t-1}\|_{2}=\sqrt{2(1-s_{t,l})}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\hat{C}_{t}-\hat{C}_{t-1}\|_{2}=\sqrt{2(1-s_{t,l})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider the function <math id="A5.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)=\frac{W_{o}x}{\|W_{o}x\|_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)=\frac{W_{o}x}{\|W_{o}x\|_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> which maps a unit vector <math id="A5.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> to the normalized output space. The approximation error <math id="A5.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the change in this map’s output. Standard results in numerical linear algebra establish that the local Lipschitz constant of the map <math id="A5.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\mapsto Wx/\|Wx\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\mapsto Wx/\|Wx\|</annotation></semantics></math> on the unit sphere is bounded by the condition number <math id="A5.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(W_{o})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(W_{o})</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, for any unit vectors <math id="A5.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u,v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u,v</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="A5.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\frac{W_{o}u}{\|W_{o}u\|}-\frac{W_{o}v}{\|W_{o}v\|}\right\|_{2}\leq\kappa(W_{o})\|u-v\|_{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\frac{W_{o}u}{\|W_{o}u\|}-\frac{W_{o}v}{\|W_{o}v\|}\right\|_{2}\leq\kappa(W_{o})\|u-v\|_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Applying this inequality to our inputs <math id="A5.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}_{t},\hat{C}_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}_{t},\hat{C}_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and outputs <math id="A5.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{Y}_{t},\hat{Y}_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{Y}_{t},\hat{Y}_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> (where <math id="A5.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> is the normalized output):</span></p>
<table id="A5.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=\|\hat{Y}_{t}-\hat{Y}_{t-1}\|_{2}\leq\kappa(W_{o})\|\hat{C}_{t}-\hat{C}_{t-1}\|_{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=\|\hat{Y}_{t}-\hat{Y}_{t-1}\|_{2}\leq\kappa(W_{o})\|\hat{C}_{t}-\hat{C}_{t-1}\|_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Substituting the expression for the input distance:</span></p>
<table id="A5.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\leq\kappa(W_{o})\sqrt{2(1-s_{t,l})}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\leq\kappa(W_{o})\sqrt{2(1-s_{t,l})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">This confirms that the upper bound holds strictly without relying on small-angle approximations.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Other Related Works</h2>

<section id="A6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.1 </span>Confidence Aware Decoding</h3>

<div id="A6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Confidence-aware decoding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2026)</a></cite> employs a dynamic decoding strategy that is independent of the predefined number of denoising steps <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.
Specifically, it unmasks any positions whose confidence score exceeds a user-specified threshold during the generation process.
This approach represents a distinct optimization scope compared to the architectural or caching strategies.
It operates strictly at the sampling level, utilizing the model’s output logit to determine tokens to decode.
Because this optimization is model-agnostic, it can be seamlessly integrated not only with Fast-dLLM but also with our proposed method and any other aforementioned frameworks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.2 </span>Sparse Attention</h3>

<div id="A6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To address the quadratic complexity of attention operations in AR LLMs, various sparse attention mechanisms have been proposed.
These methods typically leverage the observation that attention scores are highly localized, with tokens primarily attending to their neighbors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Child et al. (2019)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Beltagy et al. (2020)</a></cite>.
Beyond static patterns, recent works have focused on runtime sparsity, analyzing dynamic attention patterns to prune less critical computations during inference.
For instance, H2O <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2023)</a></cite> and Deja Vu <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2023)</a></cite> discard tokens with low accumulated attention scores (KV cache eviction) or predict contextual sparsity to skip blocks, while MInference <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al. (2024)</a></cite> identifies specific sparse patterns to accelerate the prefill stage.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While these methods primarily explore sparsity in autoregressive models, similar ideas have also been applied to diffusion LLMs.
During inference of Diffusion LLMs, only a small subset of tokens changes at each leading to substantial redundancy in attention score activations across adjacent steps.
SparseD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2026)</a></cite> exploits this property by computing full attention in early steps and, after a pre-defined step, constructing a binary attention mask by selecting high-scoring attention score blocks.
This mask is then reused in subsequent steps, allowing the model to perform efficient sparse operations for the remainder of the denoising steps.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</article>
</div>
